{
  "reference": "1 Peter 3",
  "book": "1 Peter",
  "chapter": 3,
  "status": "pending",
  "sections": [
    {
      "kind": "cultural_context",
      "title": "Household Instructions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_1",
          "text": "Verses 1–7 address wives and husbands within a household setting marked by unequal patriarchal expectations. The passage also directly charges husbands to live considerately and give honor to their wives as fellow heirs of life’s grace. Sarah’s language of obedience belongs to the passage’s scriptural and household rhetoric; it should not be taken to establish male superiority or to define women’s standing only through deference to men.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 3:1-7", "1 Peter 3:15-16", "1 Peter 3:5-6", "1 Peter 3:8-12"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_9d9bc19b305ad2391788e077"],
          "confidence": "low",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "things_easy_to_miss",
      "title": "Conduct, Blessing, and Hope",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_2",
          "text": "The call not to return evil or insult for evil is paired with a call to bless. Likewise, the requested answer in verses 15–16 is an account of the hope believers hold, given with gentleness, respect, and a good conscience. The chapter therefore joins public speech about hope to a nonretaliatory manner of life.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 3:15-16", "1 Peter 3:8-9"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_9f1f68596aa0b6d5d83cd9f3"],
          "confidence": "high",
          "interpretation_level": "fact"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "why_it_matters",
      "title": "Reading the Instructions Responsibly",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_3",
          "text": "The instructions about submission, blessing, and giving an answer should not be used to excuse coercion, abuse, victim-blaming, or pressure to remain in danger. Nor does refusing retaliation rule out boundaries, truthful reporting, legal protection, communal discipline, or solidarity with victims. Gentleness and respect in speaking about hope also do not authorize harassment, forced conversion, or contempt.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 3:1-7", "1 Peter 3:15-16", "1 Peter 3:5-6", "1 Peter 3:8-12"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_e4d3c5e7c9586337b9034800"],
          "confidence": "low",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "historical_context",
      "title": "Suffering and Opposition",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_4",
          "text": "The chapter assumes that believers may be slandered and may suffer for doing good. The letter attests social hostility, but it does not identify a particular emperor or describe an empire-wide persecution decree. The passage should therefore be read as addressing real opposition without attaching it to a specific official campaign not named here.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 3:1-7", "1 Peter 3:16", "1 Peter 3:18-20", "1 Peter 3:20-21"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_65ade7eee2f804d70bd39c85"],
          "confidence": "high",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "interpretive_questions",
      "title": "The Difficult Closing Passage",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_5",
          "text": "Verses 18–20 contain several long-disputed expressions. “Made alive in the spirit” may refer to life in the Spirit, being made alive by the Spirit, or a contrast between modes of existence. The “spirits in prison” have been understood as fallen angels, dead people from Noah’s generation, or other spiritual beings; Christ’s proclamation has likewise been read as victory, judgment, or good news. None of these readings is effortless, so the passage calls for caution rather than speculative claims about spirits or practices of deliverance.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 3:18", "1 Peter 3:19-20", "1 Peter 3:20-22", "1 Peter 3:21"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_d6298f374120173335b8c08d"],
          "confidence": "low",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        },
        {
          "id": "block_6",
          "text": "The Noah comparison says that eight people were brought safely through water and then describes baptism as a corresponding antitype. Yet the text distinguishes baptism from merely removing bodily dirt and relates it to conscience and to Christ’s resurrection. Christians have understood “baptism now saves” in sacramental, covenantal, symbolic, and faith-response ways. The verse should not be treated as a magical guarantee, a basis for forced baptism, or a denial of grace to those unable to receive the rite.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 3:18", "1 Peter 3:19-20", "1 Peter 3:20-22", "1 Peter 3:21"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_d6298f374120173335b8c08d"],
          "confidence": "low",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "surrounding_passages",
      "title": "Within the Letter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_7",
          "text": "First Peter is a coherent letter that brings together blessing, ethical exhortation, scriptural interpretation, household discourse, and apocalyptic consolation. Chapter 3 reflects this wider pattern as it moves from household relationships and communal conduct to suffering, Christ’s proclamation, Noah, baptism, and Christ’s exaltation.",
          "verse_refs": ["1 Peter 1:1-25", "1 Peter 2:1-25", "1 Peter 4:1-19", "1 Peter 5:1-14"],
          "provenance_refs": ["reader_path_1_peter_003_6fa9d6e99823629012a2ae63"],
          "confidence": "high",
          "interpretation_level": "disputed"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "generated_metadata": null
}